      "body": "VLLM_MOE_SKIP_PADDING (enabled by default) lets the topk kernels mark cudagraph padding rows by writing -1 into topk_ids, so downstream MoE kernels can skip work for them. The AITER fused-expert path never handled that sentinel and passed topk_ids straight into aiter.fused_moe, where moe_sorting indexes the expert weights with the raw id. A value of -1 reads outside the weight buffer and trips a GPU memory access fault:\r\n\r\n  Memory access fault by GPU node-9 on address 0x7f255b5b2000.\r\n  Reason: Unknown.\r\n\r\nThis reproduces on DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash (MXFP4 experts, TP=4, MI355X): the profiling run builds a fully synthetic batch whose rows are all marked as padding, so every routing index is -1 and all ranks fault before the server can start. It is not limited to startup -- trailing cudagraph padding rows are marked the same way on real batches.\r\n\r\nNeutralize the sentinel where the other AITER dtype fixups happen: route padding rows to expert 0 with zero weight, contributing nothing to the output. The rewrite is branchless so it remains cudagraph-capturable.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Purpose\r\n\r\n## Test Plan\r\n\r\n## Test Result\r\n\r\n---\r\n<details>\r\n<summary> Essential Elements of an Effective PR Description Checklist </summary>\r\n\r\n- [ ] The purpose of the PR, such as \"Fix some issue (link existing issues this PR will resolve)\".\r\n- [ ] The test plan, such as providing test command.\r\n- [ ] The test results, such as pasting the results comparison before and after, or e2e results\r\n- [ ] (Optional) The necessary documentation update, such as updating `supported_models.md` and `examples` for a new model.\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n",
      "changedFiles": 2,
